Re-target pyjnius's Android backend from python-for-android/SDL to Flet's own
JNI bridge, libpyjni.

Upstream pyjnius assumes it is being built by python-for-android for a
kivy/SDL app: on Android it links `libSDL2.so` and asks SDL for the JNI
environment. A Flet app has no SDL. `recipes/flet-libpyjni` builds
flet-dev/libpyjni in its place — a small shared library (`libpyjni.so`)
exporting exactly two entry points:

    void   *PyJni_AndroidGetJNIEnv(void);
    jclass  PyJni_FindClass(const char *className);

`PyJni_AndroidGetJNIEnv` attaches the calling thread to the JavaVM it cached
in `JNI_OnLoad` — SDL's job upstream. `PyJni_FindClass` resolves a class
through the *application's* ClassLoader, which libpyjni also caches in
`JNI_OnLoad` (`ActivityThread.currentApplication().getClassLoader()`). That
second one matters because `JNIEnv->FindClass` takes its class loader from
the current native call frame; on a thread attached from native code there
is no frame, so the system loader is used, it only sees framework classes,
and every lookup of an app or plugin class fails. The recipe declares
`flet-libpyjni` in `requirements.host`, so `fix_wheel` adds a `Requires-Dist`
and `libpyjni.so` reaches the APK's `jniLibs/<abi>/`.

The hunks:

  1. `jnius/env.py` — `AndroidJavaLocation.get_libraries()` returns
     `['log', 'pyjni']` instead of `['SDL2', 'log']`. That list becomes
     `Extension(libraries=…)`, i.e. `-llog -lpyjni`. Without it the link
     fails outright: there is no `libSDL2.so` in the cross prefix, and the
     `PyJni_*` symbols used by the hunks below are undefined.

  2. `jnius/jnius_jvm_android.pxi` — declares the two libpyjni entry points
     and makes `get_platform_jnienv()` call `PyJni_AndroidGetJNIEnv()` rather
     than `SDL_AndroidGetJNIEnv()`. The declaration spells the return type of
     `PyJni_FindClass` as `jclass *` where libpyjni's header says `jclass`;
     `jnius/jni.pxi` typedefs `jclass` to `void *`, so both are the same
     pointer once compiled and the mismatch is harmless — but do not read
     that line as documentation of libpyjni's real signature.

  3. `jnius_conversion.pxi`, `jnius_export_class.pxi`,
     `jnius_export_func.pxi`, `jnius_utils.pxi` — route through
     `PyJni_FindClass` every lookup whose class name comes from the caller:
     `autoclass()`/`find_javaclass()` targets, signature strings, and
     `__javaclass__` / `__javabaseclass__` / `__javainterfaces__`. The
     hard-coded `java/lang/*` lookups are deliberately left on
     `j_env[0].FindClass` — the system loader resolves framework classes
     fine. (`org/jnius/NativeInvocationHandler`, used by the
     `PythonJavaClass` proxy path, is also left untouched.)

  4. `setup.py` — replace the platform sniff with a flat
     `PLATFORM = 'android'`. Upstream only concludes "android" when
     python-for-android's `NDKPLATFORM` *and* `LIBLINK` are both in the
     environment; forge sets neither, so the build would take the host
     branch: `PLATFORM` would be `darwin`, the generated `config.pxi` would
     say `DEF JNIUS_PLATFORM = 'darwin'`, `jnius.pyx` would include
     `jnius_jvm_dlopen.pxi` (which dlopens a `libjvm` at runtime — there is
     none on Android), and `get_java_setup()` would hand the compiler the
     build host's JDK include/lib dirs. Forcing `android` selects
     `AndroidJavaLocation` (empty include/lib dirs; `jni.h` comes from the
     NDK sysroot forge already puts on the include path) and
     `jnius_jvm_android.pxi` — which is also what makes hunk 3 compile at
     all, since `PyJni_FindClass` is declared only there. The other half of
     the deleted block, `PYX_FILES = [fn[:-3] + 'c' …]`, is p4a's "use the
     pre-cythonised sources" shortcut; the sdist ships only `jnius.pyx` and
     the `.pxi` files, so keeping it would look for a `jnius/jnius.c` that
     does not exist. The recipe cythonises instead
     (`requirements.build: Cython <3.1`).

The recipe is `platforms: [android]` — there is no iOS counterpart (pyobjus
covers that side). A JDK is still required on the *build* host: `setup.py`
compiles `NativeInvocationHandler.java` with the host `javac`, and this patch
leaves that alone.

Provenance: the reasoning above is reconstructed from the diff, from
libpyjni's own source (`src/pyjni.c`, flet-dev/libpyjni v1.0.1) and from the
recipe's `requirements.host`. The commits that introduced the patch ("Added
flet-libpyjni, re-build pyjnius", PR #22) record no rationale of their own.

